<p>A query inside another query introduces a new promise: this part supplies one value, this part supplies rows, or this part answers whether a match exists. The SQL can be well formed while those promises are wrong. Two receipts cannot occupy one scalar position; an unlinked receipt test can mark every order as paid; removing duplicate rows can remove legitimate contributions.</p>
<p>We will extend the January follow-up report from <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/#checkable-stages">Working with SQL</a>. Its result means one open January order with a positive or unknown balance. Order 101 owes 15 and has receipts; 102 owes 20 and has none; 106 has no line value yet, so its balance is NULL. We keep those identities and meanings while changing how a query supplies a value, selects members or becomes a shared interface.</p>
<p>The examples use <strong>PostgreSQL 18</strong>, executed on native PostgreSQL 18.6. The figures calculate logical models of their small inputs; their steps explain the answer, not the engine’s execution strategy. Read the parent’s small staged query first if <code>WITH name AS (...)</code> is unfamiliar. <a href="/review/relational-next/joins-and-aggregation/">Joining and summarising data</a> owns the join and aggregate repairs; this article concentrates on the contracts introduced by composition.</p>

