<section aria-labelledby="choose-what-the-inner-query-must-return">
 <h2 id="choose-what-the-inner-query-must-return">Choose what the inner query must return</h2>
 <p>A <strong>subquery</strong> is a query used inside another statement. Its position decides what it must supply. A selected expression needs one value. A subquery in FROM supplies columns and any number of rows. EXISTS needs only to know whether any row qualifies.</p>
 <p>The parent’s fixture makes that difference easy to check. Order 100 has receipt 200 for 10; 101 has receipts 201 for 10 and 202 for 5; 102 has none. Every stored payment amount is non-null. Keep using the parent’s disposable database, or recreate its unchanged fixture below in a fresh one.</p>
 <details><summary>Complete parent fixture for a fresh disposable database</summary><p>This drops and recreates the four exercise tables. Run it only in a fresh exercise database, before creating the view later in this article.</p><Sql code={setup} label="Orders, lines and receipts from Working with SQL" /></details>
 <p>Suppose the application asks for “the receipt amount”. The parentheses below occupy a <strong>scalar</strong> position: one column and at most one inner row. Start with 100, then substitute 102 and 101. Predict whether the outer row survives each change.</p>
 <Sql code={scalar} label="A scalar receipt lookup, with three different cardinalities" />
 <CompositionFigure scene="scalar" number={1} />
 <p>PostgreSQL returned 10 for 100 and NULL for 102, retaining one outer result row in both cases. For 101 it failed the statement with SQLSTATE <code>21000</code>: more than one row returned by a subquery used as an expression. It did not pick a receipt. These are <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-expressions.html#SQL-SYNTAX-SCALAR-SUBQUERIES">PostgreSQL’s scalar-subquery rules</a>; SQLite instead uses the first returned row, so the same ambiguity can run without an error there.</p>
 <p>A null scalar result does not prove the inner query was empty. Switch the figure to <em>One inner NULL</em>; this separate nullable input has one row, but its scalar value is also NULL:</p>
 <Sql code={nullScalar} label="One row containing NULL and no rows can yield the same scalar value" />
 <p>Establish the row bound before using a scalar lookup. The customer primary key makes the next lookup return at most one customer; the order’s non-null foreign key requires that customer to exist. Neither guarantee follows merely from calling the column customer_id.</p>
 <Sql code={lookup} label="A declared key establishes the scalar row bound" />
 <p>Payments have no unique constraint on order_id: several receipts are allowed. If the requirement is total paid, ask for SUM. If it is the smallest receipt, ask for MIN and call the column smallest_receipt. If it is the most recent receipt, first define the event time and a tie-breaker, then make an ordered selection. An unexplained LIMIT 1 replaces a detectable ambiguity with a silently chosen value. <a href="/review/relational-next/window-functions/">Calculating across related rows</a> develops deliberate latest-row choices.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="make-the-dependency-on-the-outer-row-visible">
 <h2 id="make-the-dependency-on-the-outer-row-visible">Make the dependency on the outer row visible</h2>
 <p>“Total paid” is still incomplete. Total paid for this order, or across the whole shop? A <strong>correlated subquery</strong> refers to a value from an enclosing query. Here <code>o.order_id</code> supplies that dependency; <code>p.order_id</code> belongs to the inner payment reference.</p>
 <Sql code={correlated} label="One order, its receipt total, and whether any receipt exists" />
 <p>For 101, the predicate admits 10 and 5, so paid is 15 and has_payment is true. For 102, it admits nothing. The SUM without GROUP BY still returns one row, containing NULL, while EXISTS returns false. The parent defaults this absent payment total to zero when calculating due; here we leave it visible to inspect what the inner query supplied.</p>
 <p>Follow both outer orders in Figure 2, then remove the correlation. The receipt rows are unchanged.</p>
 <CompositionFigure scene="correlation" number={2} />
 <p>Without the link to the outer order, both rows receive the shop total, 65, and true. This version runs successfully:</p>
 <Sql code={global} label="Wrong for a per-order answer: both inner questions are global" />
 <p>Qualification is a correctness aid. Write <code>p.order_id = o.order_id</code>, not a pair of unqualified names whose scope you have to guess. A typo such as <code>p.order_id = p.order_id</code> asks a different question even though it looks like a relationship test. Correlation describes which outer values affect the answer. It does not establish how many physical subquery executions PostgreSQL performs; that belongs to <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">performance diagnosis</a>.</p>
 <p>A table subquery has a different interface. This one supplies two columns and one subtotal row per order that has payments. Its alias r names that result. A left join retains an order without a subtotal:</p>
 <Sql code={derived} label="A table-shaped result with an explicit one-row-per-order contract" />
 <p>It returns <code>(101, 15)</code> and <code>(102, NULL)</code>, the same order/paid pairs as the scalar aggregate. The grouping establishes the bound on matches. Joining raw receipts instead gives two rows for 101 and none for 102:</p>
 <details><summary>The raw join that changes the result-row meaning</summary><Sql code={rawJoin} label="One order/receipt pair per row" /></details>
 <p>Switch the figure to <em>Join raw receipts</em> to see those pairs. Replacing EXISTS with a join has the same danger: several matches can repeat an outer row. A left join repairs preservation, but still repeats 101. Choose the result-row meaning first; use <a href="/review/relational-next/joins-and-aggregation/#independent-totals">Joining and summarising data</a> when that meaning requires combining independently repeated details.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="define-what-membership-means-when-values-can-be-missing">
 <h2 id="define-what-membership-means-when-values-can-be-missing">Define what membership means when values can be missing</h2>
 <p>The follow-up list may need to exclude customers handled elsewhere. Our stored customer keys cannot be null, but an imported candidate list or an outer join can supply a missing key. Use a separate tiny fixture to make that boundary visible: candidates are 1, 2 and NULL; blocked keys are 1, 1 and NULL. Repeating 1 should not repeat a candidate.</p>
 <p><code>IN</code> asks whether any inner value equals the candidate. A correlated EXISTS asks whether any row passes its comparison. Both select candidate 1 here. Their negations reveal a difference. Before applying WHERE, predict <code>2 NOT IN (1, 1, NULL)</code>: two comparisons establish inequality, but the missing value cannot establish it. The answer is unknown, represented by SQL NULL.</p>
 <CompositionFigure scene="membership" number={3} />
 <p>With the original input, NOT IN is false for 1 and unknown for both 2 and NULL. WHERE keeps only true, so it selects nothing. NOT EXISTS with ordinary equality is true for 2 and NULL: no blocked row compares equal to either. For the null candidate, that is a statement about the equality test, not proof that its unknown identity is safe to contact. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-subquery.html">subquery-expression rules</a> define this behaviour; <a href="/review/relational-next/sql-values/#follow-unknown-through-a-predicate">Getting SQL values right</a> follows unknown through general expressions.</p>
 <details><summary>Runnable truth table, before filtering</summary><Sql code={membership()} label="Keep true, false and NULL visible in selected columns" /></details>
 <p>Change the blocked list to <em>1, 1</em>. Candidate 2 now has IN false and NOT IN true; the null candidate still has unknown for both. Then choose <em>Blocked: empty</em>. With no comparisons, IN is false and NOT IN true even for the null candidate. That empty-input case is easy to lose when reasoning from the slogan “NULL makes NOT IN unknown”.</p>
 <p>For this exclusion task, the policy is to contact only candidates with a known identity and no matching blocked key. Express both decisions:</p>
 <Sql code={exclusion} label="Exclude blocked keys and deliberately reject missing candidate identities" />
 <p>This returns 2. Filtering nulls only inside NOT IN does not encode the same outer-key policy for an empty inner result. Nor should every task reject nulls. If a missing value is a legitimate shared category for this task, PostgreSQL’s <code>IS NOT DISTINCT FROM</code> makes two null values compare as equal:</p>
 <Sql code={nullSafe} label="An alternative policy when two missing values must count as a match" />
 <p>The figure’s <em>Null-safe EXISTS</em> choice changes only the EXISTS correlation to this comparison. The null candidate now matches the null blocked row, so NOT EXISTS is false for it. The IN columns retain ordinary equality. These are meaning choices; no predicate rewrite here promises a faster query.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="combine-result-sets-with-the-intended-multiplicity">
 <h2 id="combine-result-sets-with-the-intended-multiplicity">Combine result sets with the intended multiplicity</h2>
 <p>Sometimes two queries select the same kind of thing for different reasons. The parent’s checked follow-up result selects orders 101, 102 and 106: customers 1, 1 and 3. A second selection finds customers attached to any recorded receipt: 1, 1, 1 and 2. We want one customer identity for either reason, including Tariq even though his order is settled.</p>
 <Sql code={customerSets} label="Two customer selections, combined into one list of identities" />
 <p>UNION returns 1, 2 and 3. INTERSECT would retain only 1, selected by both branches. EXCEPT, with follow_up on the left, would return only 3, selected by that branch and absent from the receipt branch. Swap the EXCEPT branches and the answer is 2. These operations compare complete output rows, not the source identities those rows once came from.</p>
 <p>UNION ALL retains every copy. Use it when the branches contribute separate events or amounts and equal rows remain separate contributions. To see the counting rules clearly, Figure 4 uses a deliberately repeated one-column input: A has <code>1, 1, 1, 2, NULL</code>, B has <code>1, 1, 3, NULL, NULL</code>. First predict how many copies of 1 survive EXCEPT ALL.</p>
 <CompositionFigure scene="sets" number={4} />
 <p>EXCEPT ALL retains one 1: three copies minus two. INTERSECT ALL retains two 1s, the smaller input count. UNION ALL retains five. Without ALL, UNION, INTERSECT and EXCEPT each retain at most one copy of a qualifying whole row. Notice the NULL rows: for set duplicate comparison they belong to one duplicate class, even though ordinary <code>NULL = NULL</code> is unknown. A second output column can distinguish rows that looked identical when only the first column was displayed.</p>
 <details><summary>Run the repeated-row fixture with any of the six operators</summary><Sql code={setQuery('EXCEPT ALL')} label="An explicit typed NULL, unequal multiplicities and final ordering" /></details>
 <p>Each branch must return the same number of columns, in matching positions, with types PostgreSQL can resolve together. <code>customer_id, due</code> does not line up with <code>due, customer_id</code>, even if both pairs can convert to numeric. Compatible types do not establish compatible meanings. Select columns explicitly, cast placeholders such as <code>NULL::integer</code> deliberately, and check the resulting types with pg_typeof. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/typeconv-union-case.html">PostgreSQL’s type resolution</a> applies separately to each output position.</p>
 <p>Parenthesise mixed operations to state which combined result feeds the next operation. PostgreSQL gives INTERSECT higher precedence than UNION and EXCEPT; SQLite groups its compound operators from left to right. Parentheses make this intended grouping explicit:</p>
 <Sql code={mixed} label="Combine A and B first, then subtract C" />
 <p>PostgreSQL and MySQL 8.4 support all six variants shown. SQLite and SQL Server support UNION ALL but do not offer INTERSECT ALL or EXCEPT ALL. Porting a repeated-row comparison therefore needs an explicit replacement, not just removal of ALL.</p>
 <p>Put the final ORDER BY over the complete result. Ordering a branch does not order the combined answer. A branch-local limit needs its own parentheses in PostgreSQL and defines which rows that branch contributes:</p>
 <Sql code={limited} label="Select two newest orders locally, then order the complete customer result" />
 <p>Here the first branch chooses two order rows, which may belong to the same customer; UNION then removes repeated customer identities. It is not a request for two distinct newest customers. The final ORDER BY is what displays the resulting customer IDs in order.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="give-a-checked-query-a-reusable-interface">
 <h2 id="give-a-checked-query-a-reusable-interface">Give a checked query a reusable interface</h2>
 <p>The follow-up rule is now checked: current receipts against open orders placed in January 2026, retaining positive or unknown balances. If several callers need that same meaning, give the query a name. A normal <strong>view</strong> supplies a query interface that other SELECTs can use like a table.</p>
 <p>The definition below uses the parent’s unchanged line_totals, payment_totals and balances stages. Its interface keeps the order identity, customer identity, placement time, due and receipt-presence flag. Names and explicit columns make the promise visible; a display name can still be joined by the caller.</p>
 <details><summary>Create the view from the complete checked composition</summary><Sql code={view} label="A named interface for the January 2026 follow-up rule" /></details>
 <Sql code={consume} label="A consuming query chooses its own display columns and order" />
 <p>PostgreSQL returned the original three rows: 101, Mina, 15, true; 102, Mina, 20, false; 106, Edda, NULL, false. Adding a 5-unit receipt for 101 inside a transaction changed its view result to 10; rolling back restored 15. The normal view stores its definition, rather than a saved copy of those result rows. Its name also promises neither a particular plan nor a fixed output order. The caller still supplies ORDER BY.</p>
 <p>Share a view when callers should share a stable meaning, not merely because a SQL string is long. This one deliberately fixes the placement period while including all currently visible receipts; it is not a historical balance at January’s end. <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Performance</a> owns materialised results and execution choices. <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> owns changing shared interfaces and deploying them; <a href="/review/relational-next/security/">Securing a relational service</a> owns permission design. Creating a name alone settles none of those decisions.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="follow-a-hierarchy-only-when-the-task-requires-traversal">
 <h2 id="follow-a-hierarchy-only-when-the-task-requires-traversal">Follow a hierarchy only when the task requires traversal</h2>
 <p><strong>This section is optional.</strong> Ordinary reports, window calculations, pagination and data changes do not depend on recursion. Reach for a recursive query when the requested rows are defined by repeatedly following a relationship: list every category below Kitchen, however many levels the catalogue currently has.</p>
 <p>Our small category table gives each category at most one parent. The hand-drawn tree is <code>Shop → Kitchen → Cups → Travel cups</code>, with <code>Shop → Garden</code> alongside it. The task excludes the selected root and returns its descendants, with a path from that root. For Shop, expect Kitchen, Cups, Travel cups, Garden in path order. Selecting Garden should give no descendants.</p>
 <Sql code={hierarchySetup} label="A separate five-category fixture in the disposable database" />

 <h3 id="separate-the-starting-rows-from-each-expansion">Separate the starting rows from each expansion</h3>
 <p>A recursive CTE has starting rows, called its <strong>anchor</strong>, and an expansion that refers to the preceding working rows. The anchor below selects Shop at depth zero and creates the integer path <code>[1]</code>. Each expansion follows parent_id to a child, increases depth by one and appends the child’s ID to the path. PostgreSQL’s ARRAY, array append and ANY syntax carry the visited IDs.</p>
 <Sql code={traverse()} label="Descendants of Shop, with path checking and a visible depth bound" />
 <p>The recursive columns agree in type: integer ID, text name, integer depth and integer-array path on both sides. A path is data, not a promise about the order the engine visits nodes. The final ORDER BY path establishes the display order.</p>
 <p>Play the complete tree in Figure 5. It separates the anchor from each expansion; its rounds are a logical explanation of recursion, not recorded engine work.</p>
 <CompositionFigure scene="recursion" number={5} />
 <p>The working rows are first Shop, then Kitchen and Garden, then Cups, then Travel cups. The next expansion supplies nothing, so it stops. The final result filters out depth zero and sorts the paths: <code>[1,2]</code>, <code>[1,2,4]</code>, <code>[1,2,4,5]</code>, <code>[1,3]</code>. A missing root starts with no rows; a leaf starts with one root row but produces no descendants. Try both choices in the figure.</p>

 <h3 id="stop-cycles-and-choose-which-repeats-to-retain">Stop cycles and choose which repeats to retain</h3>
 <p>A foreign key confirms that a parent exists; it does not stop a category from becoming its own ancestor. In this disposable fixture, making Shop a child of Travel cups closes the path back to Shop. Select <em>Add cycle 5 → 1</em> in the figure. The guard rejects an ID already present in that path, so it does not append Shop again. The traversal terminates; the malformed hierarchy still needs a separate integrity repair.</p>
 <p>Replacing UNION ALL with UNION is not a general cycle defence. It removes repeated complete rows, but <code>(1, depth 0)</code> and <code>(1, depth 4)</code> are different rows; a growing path also makes each revisit different. Our explicit visited-path check gives the stopping rule. PostgreSQL also offers a documented CYCLE clause for marking cycles and preventing further expansion.</p>
 <p>The depth bound is a second, different guard. Choose <em>Depth bound: 2</em>. Cups appears at depth two, but Travel cups is omitted. Cups has an unvisited child beyond the bound, so truncated is true. Check that no returned row is truncated before presenting the bounded result as the complete descendants. Raising the cap can recover a deeper answer; merely stopping at a cap does not establish completeness.</p>
 <p>Each row here represents one surviving path. In this one-parent fixture a descendant has one such path from the root. In data allowing several parents, distinct paths can reach the same node without a cycle. A per-path cycle guard would retain both. If the requirement is unique reachable nodes instead, choose that result policy explicitly and project/deduplicate node identities after defining the traversal; deduplicating entire paths will not do it.</p>
 <p>This runnable query is PostgreSQL-specific. MySQL 8.4 and SQLite also use WITH RECURSIVE, but this array path must be rewritten for their available types and operations; SQL Server’s recursive CTE syntax omits the RECURSIVE keyword and has a MAXRECURSION option. Bounds and cycle support differ. Port the stopping and multiplicity rules as well as the syntax.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="check-the-contracts-introduced-by-composition">
 <h2 id="check-the-contracts-introduced-by-composition">Check the contracts introduced by composition</h2>
 <p>Keep the tiny counterexample beside each composition. A positive result on order 101 alone would miss a lost correlation: both versions say it has payments. Order 102 exposes the difference. Likewise, a set fixture with no repeats cannot distinguish UNION from UNION ALL.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Composition checks and their expected answers"><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Expected answer and why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Scalar lookup for 100, 102, 101</td><td>10, NULL, then cardinality error. A one-row NULL input also yields NULL; check presence separately if needed.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Correlated total/presence for 101 and 102</td><td>(15, true), (NULL, false). Removing the link gives (65, true) for both.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Nullable blocked list</td><td>NOT IN keeps no candidates. Ordinary NOT EXISTS keeps 2 and NULL; the explicit non-null policy keeps only 2.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Empty blocked list</td><td>NOT IN and NOT EXISTS are true for all three candidates. Explicit rejection of a null outer identity still excludes it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Repeated set rows</td><td>UNION ALL: ten rows; UNION: four. INTERSECT ALL: two 1s and one NULL. EXCEPT ALL: one 1 and one 2.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Normal view and a rolled-back receipt</td><td>Three follow-up rows. 101’s due changes from 15 to 10, then returns to 15.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Optional traversal boundaries</td><td>Missing root and leaf: no descendants. Cycle: no repeated root in a path. Depth two: Cups flags truncation.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>Compare complete values and copy counts, including NULLs. The parent’s two-way EXCEPT ALL comparison detects missing and extra copies; it deliberately ignores order. Check output types separately, because a set comparison may coerce compatible types before comparing them. Check the explicit final order independently where the query promises one.</p>
 <p>Our native checks compared all four membership predicates for all three inner inputs, then checked null-safe correlation separately. They compared every copy returned by all six set variants, both correlated orders, the view’s values and types, and every hierarchy mode. A controlled scalar error confirmed SQLSTATE 21000. A bounded negative example revisiting the same ID with changing depth confirmed that UNION alone retained each distinct depth row.</p>
 <p>These checks establish the illustrated answers. They do not establish acceptable execution cost or prove that a refactor stays correct on data outside the stated contract. Once meaning is settled, use representative data and <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> to inspect the work required to produce it.</p>
</section>

<section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources">
 <h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
 <p>Checked on 20 October 2026. SQL results and error behaviour were executed on native PostgreSQL 18.6, with UTC and read committed, in a disposable database. Fixtures are invented. JavaScript figures were compared with those SQL answers; their logical steps are models, not observations of physical execution. Dialect comparisons below come from the named manuals, not runs of those engines.</p>
 <ul>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-expressions.html#SQL-SYNTAX-SCALAR-SUBQUERIES">Scalar Subqueries</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-subquery.html">Subquery Expressions</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-comparison.html">Comparison Functions and Operators</a>: row bounds, correlation, membership and null-safe comparison.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/queries-union.html">Combining Queries</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-select.html">SELECT</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/typeconv-union-case.html">UNION, CASE, and Related Constructs</a>: duplicate counts, precedence, column compatibility and type resolution.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createview.html">CREATE VIEW</a> for a normal view’s query definition; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/queries-with.html#QUERIES-WITH-RECURSIVE">Recursive Queries</a> for working rows, path ordering and cycle guards.</li>
  <li>SQLite: <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/lang_expr.html#subquery_expressions">Subquery Expressions</a> for first-row scalar behaviour and <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html#compound_select_statements">Compound Select Statements</a> for left-to-right grouping and available operators.</li>
  <li>MySQL 8.4: <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/set-operations.html">Set Operations</a> and <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/with.html">WITH</a>. SQL Server: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/language-elements/set-operators-except-and-intersect-transact-sql">EXCEPT and INTERSECT</a> and <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/with-common-table-expression-transact-sql">WITH common_table_expression</a>.</li>
  <li>Continue with <a href="/review/relational-next/sql-values/">Getting SQL values right</a> for expression truth and conversions, <a href="/review/relational-next/joins-and-aggregation/">Joining and summarising data</a> for the input row contract, or <a href="/review/relational-next/window-functions/">Calculating across related rows</a> for comparisons that retain detail rows.</li>
 </ul>
</section>
